{{Politics of Balisca}}
'''Zavier Bilal''' (''Zavier Amir Bilal''; born 6 August 1985 in [[Jatiel]], [[Araucaria]]) is a [[Balisca|Baliscan]] confederal delegate, currently serving as the 43rd [[Secretary General (Balisca)|Secretary General]] of Balisca, and has been a delegate since April 2020. Bilal attended the [[University of Castejón, Capitoline]], eventually earning a {{W|Bachelor of Computer Science|Bachelor's degree}} in [[w:Computer science|computer science]]. He is the youngest person to hold the positions of International Secretary, Deputy and Secretary General. He is a member of the [[Cosmopolitan Collective]], a [[List of platforms in Balisca|platform]] that supports [[Accelerationism|Baliscan accelerationist]] and [[W:Cyber-utopianism|digital socialist]] tenets.

During the [[2018 Baliscan confederal election|2018 election]], Bilal was elected to the [[Confederal Congress]] to represent [[Almuña (commune district)|Almuña]], a relatively urban commune in northeastern [[Araucaria]]. In 2020, he was {{W|nomination|recommended}} by Secretary General [[Andrea Palacios]] to be the new Secretary of International Development, and was unanimously confirmed by Congress. Following the resignation of Júlio Kayano from the  position of Deputy Secretary General, Bilal was appointed on 2 December 2020, becoming the youngest person to ever hold the position. Bilal later co-authored the $1.1 trillion [[Baliscan Green Recovery Initiative|Confederal Green Recovery Initiative]] in response to the [[w:COVID-19 pandemic|COVID-19 pandemic]], which expanded and funded the [[Green Protocol]] through 2050. He led the opposition to many of Palacios' unpopular economic, labor, and tax reform proposals, ultimately leading to him being called a {{W|fifth column}} and calls for him to be recalled or dismissed.

Bilal led the Baliscan response to West Pacific's detention of the [[Baliscan duo]], which ultimately resulted in confederal sanctions against government officials of the country. His response to the crisis was widely praised by the Baliscan public, leading to significant rises in his approval ratings. He and several other ministers later broke with Palacios and her government's official position, aligning himself with the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest|Indignados movement]]. This led to a schism within the government that forced Palacios to resign from both the Premiership and her seat in Congress on July 27th, passing the premiership to Bilal. After public consultations, elections were held in [[2021 Baliscan confederal election|October 2021]] with the [[2021 Baliscan premiership election|premiership election taking place in November]], in which Bilal was elected with the support of 61% of all votes cast.

Bilal's ideology has been self-described as "no-bullshit" with an emphasis on [[W:Communalism (Bookchin)|communalism]], [[w:Social justice|social justice]], [[w:Alter-globalization|alter-globalization]], and {{W|climate justice|climate}} and [[w:Global justice movement|global justice]]. Internationally, Bilal has signaled a much more active Baliscan policy, significantly expanding the scope of Balisca's [[W:cultural diplomacy|cultural diplomacy]], and confronting [[W:neo-nationalism|neo-nationalism]]. These policies have been criticized for straining relations with countries that Balisca has typically had cordial ties, but praised for the establishment of [[Concepción Organization of Co-Operation|COCO]] and it's advocacy for {{W|alter-globalization}} and opposition to [[w:Neonationalism|neo-nationalism]]. Bilal has moved Balisca to the forefront of international {{W|Humanitarianism|humanitarian}} efforts, and has led the movement to [[w:Reform of the United Nations|reform the United Nations]], specifically the [[w:Reform of the United Nations Security Council|United Nations Security Council]].
