==Political positions==
Zavier Bilal is a {{W|left-wing}} politician, who has been described along a wide range of positions, including [[accelerationism|Baliscan accelerationism]], {{W|communalism (Bookchin)|communalism}}, and {{W|libertarian socialism}}. In this regard, Bilal has said, “I come from the Baliscan libertarian socialist tradition, that is my ideological space of reference. I am a democrat, and I believe that democracy has to change and adapt and not petrify." {{W|The Economist}} has called Bilal "hard-left" and part of the {{W|Millennial socialism|millennial left}} "[w]ith a programme focused on {{W|social justice}}, {{W|human rights}}, {{W|the environment}} and [[W:Postgenderism|postgenderism]]". Bilal has been called a "{{W|postmodernism|postmodern leader}}".

As an anarchist, Bilal has stated that he personally desires the abolition of all states, and he has justified working for the state by the opportunity it affords to promote worthwhile ends both within Balisca and across the world. Bilal's {{w|anti-statism}} stems from wanting to preserve free public spaces independent from the state, such as Internet properties, and wanting {{W|Techno-progressivism|technological advances to be applied humanistically}} so that all, rather than a few, can reap its benefits, to the exclusion of others. Bilal's government does not follow hierarchical or bureaucratic relationships. As a general practice of "{{W|radical transparency}}", all of Bilal's meetings are recorded, transcribed, and uploaded to the [[Open Cabildo]]. Bilal also publicly responds to questions and discussions on the platform.
