===International policy===
Bilal's administration has been vocal regarding the {{W|soft coup}}s in Brazil, Honduras and Paraguay, the {{W|Israeli occupation of Palestine}} and the [[W:Gaza genocide|Gaza genocide]], the {{W|Foreign interventions by the United States|interventionism}} of the [[United States]], alongside, the repressive governments of Ortega in Nicaragua, the dictatorship in {{W|China}} and the weakening of the basic conditions of democracy in {{W|Venezuela}}". During his 2021 communal election race, he said that the {{W|2021 Nicaraguan general election}} was fraudulent and called on others to rescind support for {{W|Daniel Ortega}}'s government. Bilal was vocally critical of {{W|President of Brazil}} {{W|Jair Bolsonaro}} and his stance regarding the crimes of the {{W|military dictatorship of Brazil}} (1964–1985). He also called Bolsonaro "a danger for the environment, the region, and for humanity." As a result, relations between the two countries deteriorated, but have since recovered since the election of [[W:Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva|Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva]].

He has publicly criticized the [[South Atlantic Accords]] with [[Argentina]], but his government has signaled no intention to withdraw from the accords. Bilal has also expressed distrust towards the government of the [[West Pacific]], as a result of the detention of the [[Baliscan duo]]. Bilal has mentioned that the election of Lula and {{W|Gustavo Petro}} in their respective countries could potentially lead to the formation of an interesting axis.

Regarding relations with [[Argentina]] amidst it's ongoing [[W:Mapuche conflict|Mapuche conflict]], Bilal has openly supported their ongoing struggle on several occasions. As recently as December 2020, he has openly called for the decolonization of [[W:Araucanía (historic region)|Araucanía]] and [[W:Wallmapu|Wallmapu]]. Prior to his time in Congress, Bilal was quoted calling Argentina a "genocidal [[W:Settler colonialism|settler colonial]] state" which was violating numerous international treaties, and said that "no matter how close we are to a country, we must defend international principles and human rights." In 2020, Bilal cosigned a motion which successfully sought to recognize the historical Argentine government's actions against the Mapuche and [[W:Selknam genocide|Selknam]] as {{W|genocide}}.

Addressing fellow activists in a September 2017 rally speech, Bilal called for a diplomatic policy that emphasized greater international collaboration, adherence to international agreements such as the {{W|Paris Agreement}} and the {{W|Iran nuclear deal framework}}, and promoting international {{W|social tolerance}} and respect for {{W|human rights}} via the {{W|International Bill of Human Rights}}. He emphasized the consequences associated with global economic inequality and climate change, and urged limiting the potential use of [[Baliscan Armed Forces|Baliscan military power]] overseas, saying it "must always be a last resort". He also strongly criticized past and ongoing U.S. support for "murderous regimes" since the Cold War (such as [[W:Saudi Arabia|Saudi Arabia]], [[W:Israel|Israel]], {{W|Pahlavi Iran|Imperial Iran}}, Argentina, [[W:Sukarno|Indonesia]] and El Salvador), and said that those actions continue to make the world less safe. He also spoke critically of the European response to the {{W|European migrant crisis|migrant crisis}}, and the American [[W: United States embargo against Cuba|embargo against Cuba]].

He does not consider the {{W|United Nations}} to be an effective organization for the international community in its current form, and has called for a complete overhaul of the {{W|UN Charter}} and the abolishment of the {{W|United Nations Security Council}}, or the abolition of [[W:United Nations Security Council veto power|United Nations Security Council veto power]].
==Image==
Bilal is distinguished by his way of life, looks and ideals. He is {{W|vegan}}, has long been an advocate for {{W|total liberation}} and is a prominent critic of {{W|factory farming}}. Bilal has never owned a car, and prior to becoming Secretary General he utilized {{W|public transit}} to commute to and from his office in [[Hisbanya]]. As Secretary General, Bilal rejected moving into [[Quinta de Viñas]], instead remaining in his flat in the [[Charxes]]' [[Gistaín]] barrio. Bilal (when not in an official capacity) travels with his red [[W:Electric bicycle|e-bike]] all year long, despite rain and rare snow in [[Castejón]]. For inter-regional travel, he prefers to use the [[AVB]] high-speed rail, and for international travel, he utilizes the government-designated [[W:Airbus A330 MRTT|Airbus A330 MRTT]] "Companyero-1", although he has expressed distaste for flying.[[File:Zavier Bilal 2022.jpg|200px|thumb|Bilal following his arrival at [[Torrejón Air Base]] within the [[Castejón Metropolis]]]]Since his election, Bilal has been described as a populist with his straight-forward remarks against the [[W:Corporate capitalism|globalized corporate capitalist system]], which positioned him as a "man-of-the-people", critical to his unprecedented success in his first elections. His widespread use of social media platforms (first with {{W|Twitter}}, before moving to the [[Open Cabildo]] and [[W:Bluesky|Bluesky]]) and candid rhetoric style has led to him often being called an "anti-politician".

According to a July 2021 public opinion survey, Bilal's approval rating was 67% at the start of his premiership. In December 2021, Bilal's approval rating rose to 72%. After the [[December 2021 Confederal Address|Confederal Address]] in December 2021, Bilal's approval rating jumped to 83 percent, an all-time high for any democratically elected Baliscan SG, narrowly beating [[tba]]'s record high 73% in January 2019. By March 2022, Bilal's approval had fallen slightly to 78%. Observers see Bilal's high approval ratings as a consequence of the collapse of {{W|Pink tide|conservatism in Balisca}} following the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest|2021 unrest]], and his hardline stance against the West Pacific during the crisis surrounding the [[Baliscan duo]]. One analysis attributed Bilal's popularity, in part, to his youth, attitude, and proximity in opinion to the public, which has been labeled as populism and even demagoguery by his critics.

Former Secretary General [[Cristiano Sandoval]] credited Bilal with having "moving human rights to the international forefront", but has also criticized Bilal for straining foreign relations with several formerly friendly countries.
===Overseas===
All 25 countries covered in an September 2021 poll said they would prefer to see Bilal's reelection ahead of his opponent [[Alessandro Massagué]]. In 15 of the 25 nations, people expected relations between Balisca and the international community to decrease if Bilal remained SG. More than 22,000 people were questioned by {{W|GlobeScan}} in countries ranging from {{W|Australia}} to [[Zambezi]] and across Latin America, Europe and Asia. The margin in favor of Bilal ranged from 2% in the West Pacific to 92% in the [[Soviet Union]], while an average of 66% across the 25 countries preferred Bilal compared with 34% preferring Alcantara. Some four in ten did not express a view. A similar global poll was held by [[Confederal Institute of Statistics|ICS]], with respondents "overwhelmingly" in favor of Bilal from all 17 countries, including Mexico, the United Kingdom, Finland, Sweden, Indonesia and Spain. The West Pacific gave Bilal the lowest score among the countries polled, but still preferred Bilal over Massagué. In Australia, a poll conducted in August 2021 found that over 75% of Australians wanted Bilal to win the premiership election, while only 10% showed support for Massagué.

Similar results were found in New Duveland (65% in favor of Bilal, 11% in favor of Massagué), Okaiken (49% in favor of Bilal, 13% in favor of Massagué), France (65% in favor of Bilal, 8% in favor of Massagué), Italy (45% in favor of Bilal, 15% in favor of Massagué), Germany (67% in favor of Bilal, 6% in favor of Massagué) and the Netherlands (90% in favor of Bilal, 6% in favor of Massagué). Bilal scored higher approval ratings in all 70 countries covered in an October 2021 El Pais poll, with the most favorable scores coming from Latin American and European countries.

Polls have shown that approval ratings of Balisca in other countries have decreased since Bilal took office, including a 66 percent decrease in the [[West Pacific]], a 38 percent decrease in [[China]] and a 22 percent decrease in Argentina. On the other hand, approval ratings of Balisca in left-leaning countries have increased since he took office, especially in [[Takamaiku]], [[New Duveland]], the [[Philippines]] and the [[Soviet Union]]. In addition to this, Bilal has established close relationships with prominent foreign politicians and elected officials even before his premiership candidacy, notably with [[Volodymyr Zelensky]], the President of the [[Soviet Union]] and [[Howard Ignacio]] ([[Chief Executive of the Commonwealth (Philippines)|Chief Executive]] of the Philippines).
==Personal life==
==Controversies==
===Accusations of Anti-Americanism===
{{See also|Balisca-United States relations}}
Zavier Bilal has been a frequent target of accusations of anti-Americanism throughout his political career, particularly by commentators and political figures in the [[United States]] and its allied states. These allegations stem primarily from his outspoken critiques of U.S. foreign policy, military interventions, and the global economic order, which he has described as "inherently imperialistic and structurally exploitative."

Bilal has repeatedly condemned what he characterizes as the historical and ongoing role of the United States in supporting authoritarian regimes, destabilizing democratically elected governments, and perpetuating global inequality. In speeches and policy documents, he has singled out [[W:United States involvement in regime change in Latin America|U.S. involvement in Latin America]] during the Cold War—including support for [[W:Military coups|military coups]] in Chile, Argentina, Brazil, and Guatemala—as evidence of a pattern of "imperial violence masked as diplomacy." He has referred to this history as "a stain on the conscience of the [[W:Global North|global North]]."

In a 2019 interview with [[El País|''El País'']], Bilal described American foreign policy as being driven by "corporate interests and a permanent war economy." He further stated, "[[W:American exceptionalism|American exceptionalism]] is a mythology designed to justify domination. No country has the right to impose its way of life on others through force." These remarks were widely reported in international media and sparked diplomatic criticism from U.S. officials, who accused Bilal of undermining bilateral relations and spreading "inflammatory and ideological rhetoric."

Bilal has also condemned [[W:U.S. sanctions|U.S. sanctions]] on countries such as [[W:Cuba|Cuba]], [[W:Venezuela|Venezuela]], and [[W:Iran|Iran]], calling them "instruments of [[W:Collective punishment|collective punishment]]." He has consistently advocated for the normalization of relations with Cuba and has praised the [[W:Healthcare in Cuba|Cuban public health system]] and literacy programs as "examples of what people-first governance can achieve, even under blockade."

Bilal’s criticisms have extended to U.S. domestic policy as well, particularly its handling of immigration, healthcare, racial inequality, and policing. Following the [[W:Murder of George Floyd|murder of George Floyd]] in 2020, Bilal addressed a solidarity rally in Balisca in which he condemned systemic racism and police brutality in the U.S., calling for "international accountability for [[W:Structural racism|structural racism]] as a violation of human rights."

Despite these critiques, Bilal has maintained that his positions are rooted in [[W:Anti-imperialism|anti-imperialism]], not [[W:Anti-Americanism|anti-Americanism]]. In a 2022 address to the [[Concepción Organization of Cooperation]], he stated:
{{Quote|text="We do not oppose the American people, many of whom are themselves victims of the same systems of exclusion and violence. We oppose empire, in whatever form it takes. That is a humanist stance, not a nationalist one."|author=Zavier Bilal}}
Supporters argue that Bilal’s positions are consistent with a broader tradition of internationalist solidarity and that his criticisms are directed at government policies rather than any particular nation. Detractors, however, claim that his rhetoric has at times veered into blanket condemnations of U.S. influence and has damaged diplomatic opportunities with [[Georgetown]].

Bilal has refused to temper his language, arguing that "diplomacy without honesty is complicity." Under his administration, Balisca has continued to pursue a foreign policy rooted in what Bilal calls "[[Basic Law: Universal Humanism|radical pluralism and ethical nonalignment]]," which frequently puts it at odds with U.S.-led initiatives in global forums such as the [[W:United Nations|United Nations]] and the [[W:Organization of American States|Organization of American States]].

